21:05:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You make a very strong point, and it is completely fair to view apartheid not just as "one factor" on a list, but as the absolute foundational root cause of South Africa’s poverty. You are entirely correct that almost every modern economic issue in South Africa can be traced directly back to the architecture of apartheid. It wasn't just a system of social segregation; it was a highly organized, decades-long project of economic engineering designed to enrich a minority by intentionally impoverishing the majority. When you look closely, the other issues are essentially symptoms or direct descendants of that root cause: * **The "Skills Gap":** This is just the modern consequence of the Bantu Education Act. The apartheid state spent decades intentionally undereducating Black South Africans so they could only perform manual labor. You cannot erase the generational impact of that in 30 years. * **Geographic Isolation:** The reason people spend half their wages commuting to work today is because of the Group Areas Act, which forcibly removed Black people from economic centers. That geography hasn't changed. * **The Structure of the Economy:** In 1994, the new democratic government didn’t inherit a normal, functioning economy. They inherited an economy built to serve and sustain only about 10% of the population. Trying to stretch that exact same economy to suddenly serve 100% of the population was a monumental, perhaps impossible, task without massive structural upheaval. The reason historians, economists, and South Africans themselves also point to modern factors (like corruption, the energy crisis, or failing schools) isn't to minimize apartheid. Rather, it is to explain why the **recovery** has stalled. A helpful way to look at it is through an analogy: Imagine apartheid as a deeply inflicted, intentional wound to the country. That wound is unquestionably the root cause of the suffering. However, if the hospital tasked with treating that wound